virglrenderer: guest GPU memory follows the Mac's memory (macOS memory pressure)

What: below the budget of virgl-resource-memory-budget.patch (now only a guard
against a runaway guest) nothing is refused while macOS has memory to give.
- A dispatch source (DISPATCH_SOURCE_TYPE_MEMORYPRESSURE) follows macOS's memory
  pressure. Its handler runs on a libdispatch thread and only stores the level;
  the renderer thread acts on it from virgl_renderer_poll, where it also reads
  the level itself once a second (macOS tells only some processes about a
  warning, one at a time: on a Mac mini at "warn" no event came for minutes);
  it logs each change and, when the pressure rises, lets Apple's GL free the
  storage it still holds for deleted textures and buffers (glFinish on context 0).
- A new resource of 16 MB or more (not a screen or cursor) is checked against the
  level macOS has right now (kern.memorystatus_vm_pressure_level): refused at
  "critical", and at "warn" only when it is bigger than all the free, inactive and
  purgeable memory macOS has left (host_statistics64): short of that, more swapping
  beats a black desktop. Before a refusal the renderer finishes its GL work (on the
  context that is current: this can be in the middle of a guest's commands) and
  looks again three times over 100 ms. Small resources, screens and cursors are
  never refused for pressure.
- OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_STATUS names a status file (key=value lines: in_use_mb,
  peak_mb, budget_mb, pressure, refused, lost, lost_last, lost_recent: the last
  eight lost contexts, so a reader that looks every 2 s misses none) that
  OmacVM.app and omacvm check read. Written by rename, at most four times a second from the poll,
  at once for a refusal or a lost GPU context. lost_last is the lost context's
  name, which the guest chose: only letters, digits and . _ - are kept.
- OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_PRESSURE=off|normal|warn|critical[:AVAILABLE_MB] fixes the
  level (and the available memory) instead of asking macOS: tests only.
Why: a fixed budget is either too small for real use (2.9.0's quarter of the Mac's
memory, 4 GB on a 16 GB Mac mini, was reached at 5K with fractional scaling:
Hyprland's next buffer was refused, its context lost, the VM black) or too big to
protect an 8 GB Mac, where VM memory plus GPU memory can push macOS into heavy
swapping. macOS knows how much memory it can give; this asks it.
Threading: the dispatch handler touches only two atomics; everything else runs on
the renderer thread (QEMU's main loop), like the budget.
Upstream: not sent (macOS only; upstream has no host memory limit).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-resource-budget.c (build time, Apple's software renderer):
critical refuses a 64 MB texture after trying again (>= 90 ms) but not an 8 MB
texture or a 5K screen; warn with 100 MB left fits 64 MB and refuses 128 MB; the
status file's in use, peak, pressure, refusals and two lost contexts (a sanitised
name, the recent list). In a VM: tests/graphics/fractional-scale.sh; on the Mac
mini a fixed-cap A/B, two memory soaks and a desktop restart (PR #91).
